&RHow to use Qpbuy:                                                       
&YSyntax: Qpbuy [Variable]                                                
&ROn SW:GD there is a QP(Quest Point) System, usualy a
reward for quests or helping an immortal. Qpbuy is the                    
function used with QP to buy what you like with the points that           
you've earned.&W                                                           
~

1 REDIRECT REROUTE~
Syntax: redirect [laser/shield/engine] [laser/shield/engine/default]
 
Redirection allows you to spend a little bit laser energy, shield energy,
or engine energy to give you more energy in a different field. For example
power can be redirected from lasers to shields to give less laser shots
but a faster shield regeneration rate. And vice versa.
~

1 REMOTECLOSEBAY REMOTEOPENBAY~
&WSyntax&w: remoteclosebay [target] 
&WSyntax&w: remoteopenbay  [target] 
&w 
This command allows you to remotely open or close the bays on the 
target vessel. It requires you be a registered pilot of and be in the
same starsystem of the target. 
&w 
&WSee also&w: openbay closebay 
~

1 REMOVE_MODULE~
Syntax: remove_module [obj] [ship]

Removing a module is part of customizing any craft that you may own.
Pulling out an engine, generator, or other component that's granting a
stat will cost you that stat immediately - the ship's performance is
always rebuilt from whatever's currently installed.

There are two ways to do this. Standing beside your docked ship, use
remove_module <item> <ship name> the way you always have. Or, from
inside the ship's own engine room, just remove_module <item> - no ship
name needed. This second way works whether the ship is docked or out
flying, which matters for frigates, capital ships, and supercapitals:
those classes can never dock again once launched, so the engine room
is the only way to refit one after its first launch.

Originally installed by Dejano, and repaired by Arcturus.

See also: install_module show_modules modules
~

1 REPAIRITEM~
Syntax: repairitem [name]
Requirements: thread, oven, toolkit

This skill allows the engineer to take a crack at repairing an item
themselves instead of taking it to a repairshop. The engineer requires a
toolkit, oven, and needle to even begin repairing the items themselves,
and this skill may only be performed in a factory. Warning, if you fail to
repair the item correctly you will damage it.
~

1 REVIVE~
&WSyntax&w: revive [target] 
&WRequires&w: defibulator 
&w
&wThose that fall beyond normal aid, may need to be revived. They can't 
&wbe saved by normal means, so its time to wake up their heart with a 
&wfriendly little shock. It can only be performed on those mortally 
&wwounded. 
~

1 ROSTER~
The roster command will list all members in the clan.  This command is only
availiable to the LEADER, FIRST and SECOND of a clan (or any immortals
currently in a clan :)). 
 
The file is created/updated when a character uses the SAVE, OVERTHROW, or
RECRUIT commands (or are inducted using the INDUCT) command.  They are
removed on OUTCAST, ABDICATE or (naturally) death.
 
Any people not currently on the roster (and in a clan) will be added next
time they log in.
~

1 SABOTAGE~
&WSyntax&w: sabotage [laser/ions/drive/turret1/turret2/launcher] 
&WSyntax&w: sabotage [tlauncher/rlauncher] 
&w
Sabotage is a skill that allows you, given you are in the ship's engine
room, to set up a fatal error, that results in the disabling of a given
ship part. For example a successful sabotage against the ship's laser 
would make firing it impossible until it was repaired. Sabotage is not
detected through launch checks or status checks. Sabotage will not be
detected until the damage is incurred, or until repairship is used on a
given ship part, sabotage will be detected and removed upon a succesfull
repairship. 
&w
&WSee also&w: repairship ship_maintenance
~

1 SALVAGE SALVAGING~
&w&xSyntax&w: salvage [object] 
&w 
&wSalvaging is the fine art of reverse engineering an object to its base
parts. Because taking apart any item risks damage to its components, this
skill is not one hundred percent effective in recovering parts. Some parts
can also not be salvaged. Furthermore because of limitations in the 
codebase, it is not possible to salvage almost any pre-manufactured items.
The skill is therefore largely restricted to already engineered items. 
This does include other crafting items that are *NOT* part of engineering
such as lightsabers. 
~

1 SCRAPATTACK~
Syntax: Scrapattack

This skill allows the engineer, in a fighting stance to attempt to damage
the equipment of the opposed. If an engineer is going down, they might as
well cause some damage.
~

1 SEAL~
Syntax: seal [direction]
Syntax: seal [doorname]

Smugglers, knowing the easiest ways to get around in the galaxy, can often
come up with nifty ways to open the things that can into their path. They
also have a nifty way of figuring out how to seal them back up again.
Whether it is from breaking the lock, or slicing the locking mechanism,
they never seem to forget that it is possible to seal a door. 
~

1 SELFDESTRUCT~
Syntax: selfdestruct
Technology is indeed precious, and the price of such is often great.
When captured, the last ditch defense in military tactics is to smash the
controls of the vessel even at the cost of your own demise - if this
objective alone is completed, then death is acceptable. It may be called
'cheap,' it may be called 'crude,' but it is entirely effective. It is
unrealistic to believe that a ship would not have such a defense
to protect the ship itself. It is impossible to over-ride, the reactor
coolant will disperse and the ship itself will explode in three
sequences - on a side note, self destruction on a landed ship will cause
a massive explosion.
~

1 SETRANK~
&wSyntax: setrank add [rank]
&wSyntax: setrank [rank] [field] [value]
&w
&wFields are:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&wAuthority -The Rank's Place in the chain of command
Flags     -What being this rank entitles you to.
Name      -The Rank's Name
Wage      -Rank based wages.
&w
List of flags:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
leader       - Takes precedence, access to everything.
officer      - Summon clan guards/patrols, dismiss them too. 
withdraw     - Access to clan funds.
pilot        - Access to clan ships.
clanbuyship  - Use clan funds to purchase clan ships.
clansellship - Sell clan owned ships into clan funds.
induct       - Induct new members into the clan.
outcast      - Outcast current members from clan.
promote      - Promote member to a higher rank.
demote       - Demote members to lower ranks
empower      - Ability to transfer empowerments.
roster       - Ability to view the clan roster
locked       - Only the leader can promote, demote, or outcast
~

1 SHOW_MODULE SHOW_MODULES~
Syntax: show_modules [ship]

Lists the modules currently installed in the target craft, and how many
of its module slots are used up. Handy for deciding what to remove
before installing something new, or just checking a loadout without
digging through the standard info command.

Like install_module and remove_module, this works two ways: standing
beside your docked ship with show_modules <ship name>, or from inside
the ship's own engine room with just show_modules and no ship name -
the second way works whether the ship is docked or out flying, which
is the only way to check a frigate, capital ship, or supercapital's
loadout once it's launched, since those classes can never dock again.

See also: install_module remove_module modules
~

1 "SITUATIONAL AWARENESS" SITUATIONAL_AWARENESS SITUATIONALAWARENESS~
Situational Awareness, gives the character a chance to avoid sneak attacks,
and makes them a bit more difficult to be stolen from. It doesn't work
100% of the time, but it is a good deterrent.
~

1 SLIDE~
Syntax:  slide [object] [character]
Syntax:  slide [amount] credits [character]

Slide is just like using the give command, it is virtually a mirror image
of it. With one small difference, when one slide an object, there is a
large chance that it will go unnoticed. The ability to slide is what makes
being a thief interesting. If they do not see you give them the object,
then obviously you did not.
 
See also: Give Palm Tuck
~

1 SUBCLASS SUB-CLASS~
Syntax: subclass [subclass]

Subclasses were originally written by Nicholas "Raijen" Furniss, and are
used in this mud to give a specialization in a given class. There is a
wide variety of subclasses for all the classes, and each and every one of
them has a helpfile entry. 
 
See also: subclasses
~

1 SUBCLASSES SUB-CLASSES~
Syntax: subclasses

This command gives an accurate list of all the subclasses currently in
game.
~

1 TOGGLESIMULATOR~
Syntax: togglesimulator [on/off]

Switches a ship into (or out of) simulation mode. The ship must be
docked (on the ground) to use this command - it cannot be toggled
mid-flight. Calling it with no argument flips whatever state the ship
is currently in; 'on' or 'off' set it explicitly.

A ship in simulation mode launches into a private training instance
instead of real space - see HELP SIMULATOR for what that means. This
requires a Shipsystems check to succeed.

Public craft cannot be toggled.

See also: SIMULATOR, ENDSIMULATOR
~

1 TRANSMIT~
Syntax: transmit [comlink] message

Transmissions are a general in character channel, used to broadcast to the
select few who have tuned to your frequency. For each comlink you have,
you can have them tuned to a unique frequency to use to broadcast in your
everyday lives.
 
See also: tune
~

1 TUCK~
Syntax: tuck [object] [container]

Tuck is just like using the put command, it is virtually a mirror image of
it. With one small difference, when one tucks an object, there is a large
chance that it will go unnoticed. The ability to tuck is what makes being
a thief interesting. If they do not see you conceal the object, then
obviously you did not.
 
See also: put palm slide
~

1 TUNE~
Syntax: tune [comlink] ###.###

Tuning your comlink, allows you to hear transmissions coming from that
particular location. Any transmissions over that particular frequency can
be heard by you. For every comlink, you can have an independent frequency
to monitor and use.
 
See also: transmit
~

1 UNBIND~
&WSyntax&w: unbind [player]

Unbinding a player removes their bindings, and anyone can perform such a
task using the unbind command. You however, may not remove your own
bindings, it must be performed by another character.
 
&WSee also&w: bind
~

1 ALIAS~
Syntax: alias <word> <replacement>
        alias

Creates a personal shortcut - typing <word> afterward will run <replacement>
instead. Typing alias with no arguments lists your current aliases. The
replacement cannot contain the tilde character.
~

103 ANTIRESTORE~
Syntax: antirestore <player>
        antirestore all

Marks a player (or every connected player) as unable to be affected by
restore-type healing effects. An immortal-only safety/punishment tool.
~

103 AWARD~
Syntax: award <player> <amount>

Grants a player a number of questpoints directly. Immortal-only.
~

1 BOUNTIES~
Syntax: bounties

Lists every bounty currently posted on any player, along with the credit
amount offered for each.
~

103 BOMB~
Syntax: bomb <planet name>

Conducts a bombing run against a planet in your ship's current
starsystem, undermining its governing faction's popular support on
success. You must be in the cockpit and pilot's seat of a real
spacecraft, and the ship must not be disabled, docked, or in hyperspace.
Costs ship energy whether the run hits or misses. Has no effect on an
ungoverned planet or one flagged against capture.
~

1 BRANDISH~
Syntax: brandish

Activates the spell stored in a staff you are holding, affecting everyone
in the room. You must be holding the staff in your hand.
~

1 BREAK~
Syntax: break

A ship maneuver performed from the pilot's seat of a starfighter or other
small craft, breaking hard away from your current course. You must be in
the cockpit and in the pilot's seat of an actual spacecraft to use it.
~

1 CLANRANK~
Syntax: clanrank <player> <rank>

Sets a member's rank within your organization. You must hold sufficient
standing in the clan to promote or demote others.
~

1 CREDITS~
Displays the credits and acknowledgements for this game's development.
~

103 DELETESHIP~
Syntax: deleteship <ship name>

Permanently deletes a ship from the game, including its save file. This
cannot be undone. Immortal-only.
~

1 DETACH~
Syntax: detach

Undocks your ship from whatever it is currently docked to. You must be in
the cockpit of the ship being detached.
~

1 DIEROLL~
Syntax: dieroll <sides>

Rolls a die with the given number of sides (4 to 500) and announces the
result to yourself and the room.
~

103 DISABLED~
This command has been intentionally disabled and is not available for
use.
~

103 EXITFLAGS~
Syntax: exitflags

Lists every recognized exit flag, for reference when using redit to set
up doors, secrets, or other special exits.
~

103 FIX_CLAN_MEMBERSHIP~
Syntax: fix_clan_membership

Recalculates and corrects the stored member count for every organization
in the game, then saves them. Immortal-only maintenance command.
~

103 FIX_SKILLS~
Syntax: fix_skills <player>

Rescans and corrects a player's skill/spell table entries. Immortal-only
maintenance command, useful after content changes affecting the skill
table.
~

1 HAYMAKER~
Syntax: haymaker <target>

An unarmed combat skill: a heavy, telegraphed swing. On success it deals
solid direct damage; on failure, it leaves you briefly more vulnerable to
incoming damage before you can recover your stance.
~

103 HOTBOOT~
Syntax: hotboot
        hotboot debug

Reboots the game server in place without disconnecting any players -
every socket is preserved and reattached once the new process comes back
up. Refuses to run if anyone is in combat or in the line editor. The
'debug' option writes the hotboot state file without actually rebooting,
to verify everything would be captured correctly beforehand. Immortal-only.
~

103 "IMMCHANNEL" IMMTALK~
Syntax: immtalk <message>

Speaks on the immortal-only communication channel.
~

103 IMMDEMOTE~
Syntax: immdemote <player>

Lowers a fellow immortal's rank/authority. Immortal-only.
~

103 IMMPROMOTE~
Syntax: immpromote <player>

Raises a fellow immortal's rank/authority. Immortal-only.
~

103 KEYTYPES~
Syntax: keytypes

Lists the exit lock-related flags (locked, is a door, closed, secret,
requires swimming/flying/climbing, or is a window) for reference when
building doors with redit.
~

1 MAKEDOUBLELIGHTSABER~
Syntax: makedoublelightsaber <name>

Crafts a double-bladed lightsaber, requiring the appropriate crafting
tools and components on hand. Follow the prompts for each stage of
construction.
~

103 MAKEHELP~
Syntax: makehelp

Regenerates the web-based HTML help index and per-entry help pages from
the current help database. Immortal/builder-only.
~

1 MAKEVIBROSWORD~
Syntax: makevibrosword <name>

Crafts a vibrosword from a consumed vibroblade component plus the
appropriate crafting tools. Follow the prompts for each stage of
construction.
~

103 MEA MPECHOAT~
Syntax: mpechoat <target> <message>

Mob program command: makes the mob send a message visible only to a
specific target. For use in mob program scripts, not directly by players.
~

103 MER MPECHOAROUND~
Syntax: mpechoaround <target> <message>

Mob program command: makes the mob send a message to everyone in the room
except a specific target. For use in mob program scripts, not directly by
players.
~

103 OOC2 NEWBIECHAT~
Syntax: newbiechat <message>
        ooc2 <message>

Speaks on the newbie-help channel, intended for new player questions and
answers from more experienced players.
~

1 RANKS~
Syntax: ranks

Lists the available ranks within your organization and their relative
standing.
~

1 REMBOUNTY~
Syntax: rembounty <player>

Removes any bounty currently posted on the named player.
~

1 RENT~
There is no rent system in this game - simply save and quit; your
character and belongings persist on their own.
~

1 REPORT~
Syntax: report

Announces your current hit points and movement points to yourself and
everyone in the room.
~

103 RESET_AGE~
Syntax: reset_age <player>

Resets a player's in-game age. Immortal-only, restricted to trust level
103 and above, and only usable on players of equal or lower trust.
~

103 RESET_ALL_SHIPS~
Syntax: reset_all_ships

Strips ownership from every non-Public civilian ship in the game,
returning them to unowned status. Immortal-only, use with caution.
~

103 RESETMAXSHIPMODULES~
Syntax: resetmaxshipmodules [all] [value]

Sets the module slot capacity (maxmodules) on ships in bulk.

With no value given, each ship is set to its class default:
fighter 15, midsize 30, frigate 50, capital 75, supercapital 99
(other classes fall back to 10). With an explicit value (10-99),
every affected ship is set to that same number instead.

With no 'all', only ships currently at zero (0) slots are touched -
ships that already have a capacity keep it. With 'all', every ship in
the game is stamped. In either mode, a ship whose installed module
count would exceed the new value is skipped rather than stranding
its modules.

Slot capacity is still a per-ship value underneath, not a hard
per-class rule - use setship <ship> maxmodules <value> to adjust a
single ship afterward. Immortal-only.

See also: SETSHIP, MODULES
~

103 SETMSSP~
Syntax: setmssp <field> <value>

Sets one of the MSSP (Mud Server Status Protocol) fields reported to mud
listing sites and crawlers. Immortal-only.
~

103 SHOWWEATHER~
Syntax: showweather

Displays the current global weather conditions - atmospheric pressure and
its rate of change, sky state, and sunlight level. Immortal-only.
~

1 "SLI" SLICE~
Syntax: slice <target>

A precision blade attack available only to Swordsmen. Requires an edged
weapon.
~

103 SMITE~
Syntax: smite <player>

Immediately and severely punishes the target player. Immortal-only.
~

1 SWAP_ABILITIES~
Syntax: swap_abilities <ability 1> <ability 2>

Swaps every skill and spell currently classified under one ability guild
with every one classified under another, for the remainder of the current
session. Ability numbers: 0=Combat, 1=Piloting, 2=Engineering, 3=Hunting,
4=Smuggling, 5=Diplomacy, 6=Leadership, 7=Force, 8=Medical. Immortal-only.
This change is not saved to disk and will revert on the next reboot.
~

1 TIME~
Syntax: time

Displays the current in-game date and time.
~

103 TOGGLEIMM~
Syntax: toggleimm <player>

Marks or unmarks a player character as a known alternate character of an
immortal. Immortal-only.
~

103 TOGGLENH~
Syntax: togglenh <player>

Toggles a player's membership/access to the player council. Immortal-only.
~

103 TOGGLEUT~
Syntax: toggleut <player>

Toggles a player's access to the ultalk channel. Immortal-only.
~

103 TRAINALL~
Syntax: trainall <player>

Instantly trains every skill and spell a player is eligible to learn, up
to their current practice sessions available. Immortal-only.
~

103 TRANSFERSHIP~
Syntax: transfership <ship name>

Instantly relocates a ship from anywhere in the game to your current
location, but only works while you are standing at a registered dock
(one of a planet's Doc1a/1b/1c-style landing sites). Immortal-only.
~

103 TRANSSHIP~
Syntax: transship <ship name> <destination>

Instantly relocates a ship to a specified destination. Immortal-only.
~

1 TRAP~
Syntax: trap <object> <trigger>

Rigs an object, container, door, or room exit to trigger automatically -
on being picked, opened, closed, gotten, put into, or (for a room/exit)
simply entered. Requires an unset trap kit and sufficient skill.
~

103 TRIGGERS~
Syntax: triggers

Lists every recognized mob program trigger flag, for reference when
setting up mob programs.
~

103 TRIGTYPES~
Syntax: trigtypes

Lists every recognized push/pull trigger type, noting which ones
currently have no functional effect. For reference when setting up mob
programs.
~

1 ULTALK~
Syntax: ultalk <message>

Speaks on the ultalk channel. Access to this channel must be granted by
an immortal via toggleut.
~

0 VERSION~
Syntax: version

Displays the game's current version and changelog information.
~

103 VIEWSKILLS~
Syntax: viewskills <player>

Displays a player's full list of learned skills and spells along with
their current percentages. Immortal-only.
~
1 BURN AFTERBURNER~
&BSyntax: burn
Syntax: burn off

The burn skill lets a pilot engage the ship's afterburners for a modest
boost in maximum speed. Afterburners consume extra fuel when activated
and cannot be used while an overdrive node is running. Turning them off
lets the ship settle back to normal engine output.

See also: OVERDRIVE, SPEED
~
1 FRIGATES~
Frigates are warships that sit between midships and capital ships in
size and firepower. Piloting one requires the frigates skill; without
training, a would-be pilot will not be able to work the controls for
launching, maneuvering, or hyperspace jumps.

See also: STARFIGHTERS, MIDSHIPS, 'CAPITAL SHIPS', 'SUPERCAPITAL SHIPS'
~
1 'SUPERCAPITAL SHIPS'~
Supercapital ships are the largest vessels in the galaxy - massive
command ships and dreadnoughts beyond even capital scale. Only pilots
trained in the supercapital ships skill can operate them, and their
sheer bulk makes them ponderous to maneuver despite their overwhelming
firepower and armor.

See also: 'CAPITAL SHIPS', FRIGATES
~

0 SURVIVOR~
&WSurvivor (Subclass)&w
Syntax: subclass survivor

Requires level 100 and at least level 25 in medical.

Survival experts recover hitpoints and movement faster than anyone
else and are notably harder to poison, paralyze, or otherwise put
down with unpleasant effects.

See also: SUBCLASS, SUBCLASSES
~

0 'JURY RIGGER' JURYRIGGER~
&WJury Rigger (Subclass)&w
Syntax: subclass jury rigger

Requires level 100 and at least level 100 in bounty hunting.

Masters of impromptu work, jury riggers can craft anywhere - no
factory, workshop, or quiet meditation chamber required.

See also: SUBCLASS, SUBCLASSES, CRAFTING
~

0 'CERTIFIED GENIUS' GENIUS~
&WCertified Genius (Subclass)&w
Syntax: subclass certified genius

Requires level 100, at least level 25 in medical, and at least
level 25 in engineering.

Certified geniuses can train their engineering and medical skills
25 levels beyond the normal maximum.

See also: SUBCLASS, SUBCLASSES
~

0 MERCENARY~
&WMercenary (Subclass)&w
Syntax: subclass mercenary

Requires level 100 and at least level 100 in combat.

Mercenaries can train the spray skill 50 points past perfect,
laying down suppressive fire like no one else.

See also: SUBCLASS, SUBCLASSES, SPRAY
~

1 AUTOCANNON~
Syntax: fire autocannon

Fired from the gunnery seat, a ship's autocannon unleashes a rapid
streak of shots at the current target, hammering away until the first
miss. It has a shorter range than lasers, and on fighters and
midships it can only fire in a forward arc, so keep your nose on the
target. Each shot consumes a round of ammunition; prepship restocks
the magazine at 500 credits per round.

Fighter pilots (the subclass) find their autocannon hits both harder
and more often.

Only ships fitted with an autocannon can use this - fittings are
handled by the administration.

See also: FIRE, PREPSHIP
~

1 MODULES 'SHIP MODULES'~
&WShip Modules&w

Ship modules let owners customize a hull with physical components:
engines, power generators, hull assemblies, shield generators, laser
batteries, launchers, armor plating, sensors, and more. Components
come in hull-matched sizes - fighter parts fit fighters, capital
parts fit capital ships.

Every modularized ship has a slot capacity (at least 10) shown by
show_modules. Once any module is installed, the ship's performance is
rebuilt from its components, so a ship is only as good as what is
bolted into it. Engines, generators, hull assemblies, laser batteries,
and cloaking devices can only be fitted once per ship; most other
components stack.

Menari Spaceport's Ship Upgrade Store sells every component type,
sorted into five bays by hull class - a droid at the entrance will
point you to the right one.

install_module, remove_module, and show_modules all work two ways:
standing beside your docked ship with a ship name, or from inside the
ship's own engine room with no ship name needed. The engine room way
works whether the ship is docked or out flying - the only option once
a frigate, capital ship, or supercapital has launched, since those
classes can never dock again.

For what each component type actually does, see HELP MODULE TYPES.

Commands: install_module, remove_module, show_modules
Immortal: setship <ship> maxmodules <10-99>, resetmaxshipmodules
~

1 'MODULE TYPES' MODULETYPES~
&WShip Module Types&w

What each ship module component actually does once installed:

Hyperdrive Assembly  - hyperspace jump speed
Engine               - realspace speed
Shield Generator      - maximum shield strength
Fuel Cell            - maximum energy (fuel) capacity
Missile Launcher     - adds missile launcher(s) and missile capacity
Torpedo Launcher     - adds torpedo launcher(s) and torpedo capacity
Rocket Launcher      - adds rocket launcher(s) and rocket capacity
Communications Array - comm rating (best installed wins, does not stack)
Sensor Array         - sensor rating (best installed wins, does not stack)
Astrogation Array    - astrogation rating (best installed wins)
Chaff Dispenser      - maximum chaff capacity
Maneuvering Thruster - maneuverability
Laser Focusing Crystal - laser damage per hit
Ion Cannon           - number of ion cannons
Armor Plating        - armor rating
Cargo Pod            - maximum cargo capacity
Tractor Beam Emitter - tractor beam strength
Laser Battery        - adds laser cannon(s) (one battery per ship)
Overdrive Booster    - enables ship overdrive (one per ship)
Cloaking Device      - enables ship cloaking (one per ship)

A ship with zero modules installed keeps whatever stats an immortal
set on it directly with setship. The moment you install even one
module, every stat above is rebuilt from scratch out of your
installed components - stats setship gave you that aren't backed by
a module (such as hull strength) are unaffected either way.

See also: MODULES, INSTALL_MODULE, SHOW_MODULES
~

1 ENDSIMULATOR~
Syntax: endsimulator

Used from the cockpit of a ship currently running in simulation mode,
this ends the training run and pulls the ship back out - it returns
to the last real dock it launched from, is fully repaired, refueled,
and rearmed in the process, and its hatch opens automatically.

Ending a run does NOT turn simulation mode off by itself - the ship
will re-enter the simulator the next time it launches. Use
togglesimulator off once you're back on the ground if you want the
ship to fly in real space again.

See also: SIMULATOR, TOGGLESIMULATOR
~

1 SIMULATOR 'SIMULATION MODE'~
&WShip Simulators&w

Any owned ship can be switched into simulation mode with
togglesimulator while docked. A ship in this mode launches into a
private training instance instead of real space - nothing you do
there is real. Weapons fire, damage, and destruction all happen
normally, but a 'destroyed' simulator ship simply ends the run rather
than being lost, so it's a safe place to drill combat, practice
piloting, or test a loadout.

Because it isn't a real flight, a simulator run does not expose the
rest of the galaxy - calculate and plot will not show you real
starsystems, and jumps to them are refused, while the run is active.

Leave the simulator with endsimulator, which returns your ship to
the dock it launched from, fully repaired and restocked. Simulation
mode itself stays on until you explicitly turn it off with
togglesimulator off - ending a run doesn't do that for you.

See also: TOGGLESIMULATOR, ENDSIMULATOR
~

1 SPRAY~
Syntax: spray <direction> <rounds>
        spray <person> <rounds>

Lays down sustained automatic fire from a blaster or bowcaster, either
at someone standing right in front of you or blindly through an exit
at whoever's unlucky enough to be on the other side. Not usable in a
safe room.

Each round fired is an independent chance to hit, and that chance gets
worse as the burst goes on - your aim isn't getting better by holding
the trigger down. Dual-wielded automatic weapons fire both at once,
splitting your base chance between them. Ammo is spent per shot fired,
whether that shot hits or not, and a weapon that runs dry mid-burst
just clicks uselessly for the rest of the rounds you asked for.

Mercenaries train this further than anyone else.

See also: FIRE, HAYMAKER
~

1 BOARD~
Syntax: board <ship name>
        board dock

Enters a ship sitting in the room with you, through its open hatch.

The second form, 'board dock', is different: used from your own
ship's entrance while your ship is fully docked to another one (see
DOCKING), it crosses straight into whatever ship you're docked to -
no open hatch required, since you're passing through a sealed docking
connection rather than stepping out into vacuum. This only works once
docking is fully complete; partway through, it'll tell you to wait.

See also: LEAVESHIP, DOCKING, TRACTORBEAM
~

0 $~

